Objective
Configure a centralized time synchronization architecture:
Internet NTP → Ubuntu Chrony Server → All Endpoints (Windows + Linux)
This ensures consistent timestamps across systems for accurate log correlation in SIEM platforms like Wazuh.

1. Install Chrony (Ubuntu Server)
sudo apt update
sudo apt install chrony -y

2. Configure Chrony Server
# Edit the configuration file:
## sudo nano /etc/chrony/chrony.conf

Recommended Configuration
# Use trusted internet NTP sources (upstream)
pool ntp.ubuntu.com iburst

# Allow internal network clients
allow 192.168.x.0/24

# Optional: logging
logdir /var/log/chrony

Notes
pool → upstream internet time sources
allow → permits clients in your network to sync

3. Restart and Enable Chrony
sudo systemctl restart chrony
sudo systemctl enable chrony

4. Verify Chrony Server Status
chronyc tracking
Expected Output:
Reference ID → external NTP server (e.g., tock.espanix.net)
Stratum → 2 (normal)
Leap status → Normal
chronyc sources
Expected Output:
^* ntp-server (active source)

5. Configure Linux Clients

Edit client configuration:

sudo nano /etc/chrony/chrony.conf
Replace existing pools with:
server 192.168.x.xx iburst

Restart Chrony:
sudo systemctl restart chrony

Verify client sync:
chronyc sources
Expected:
^* 192.168.x.xx

6. Configure Windows Clients
Open Command Prompt as Administrator and run:
w32tm /config /manualpeerlist:"192.168.x.xx" /syncfromflags:manual /update
net stop w32time
net start w32time
w32tm /resync

Verify:
w32tm /query /status
Expected:
Source: 192.168.x.xx
Stratum: 3

7. Validate Time Synchronization Across Systems
On Linux:
date
On Windows:
time /T

All systems should have nearly identical time.

8. Verify Time Hierarchy
On Linux Clients:
chronyc sources
Expected:
^* 192.168.1.51

On Chrony Server:
chronyc tracking
Expected:
Reference ID → external NTP

On Windows:
w32tm /query /status
Expected:
Source: 192.168.x.xx

Troubleshooting
Error: 506 Cannot talk to daemon
sudo systemctl start chrony
Chrony fails to start

Check logs:
journalctl -xeu chrony.service
Wrong time source on client

Ensure:

No external pool lines remain
Correct server IP is configured

SOC Considerations

In a SOC environment:
# All endpoints must use a single trusted time source
# Time drift can indicate:
# misconfiguration
# VM rollback
# attacker tampering

Accurate time is critical for:
Event correlation
Incident response
Timeline reconstruction

Final Architecture
Internet NTP
     ↓
Ubuntu Chrony Server (192.168.x.xx)
     ↓
Windows + Linux Clients
     ↓
SIEM (Wazuh)
Outcome
Centralized time synchronization
Consistent logs across all endpoints
